      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nEnrolls three long LM eval steps and two entrypoints AMD mirrors in `automatic_shard`. Two of the eval steps were taken out of #61473 because they failed on main at the time; main passes them again now. `lm-eval-humming-h100-default` also moves from three per-shard config lists to one combined list, as #62120 did for the MoE Refactor steps.\n\n## Claims\n\n| Step | Was | Planned shards |\n|---|---|---:|\n| `lm-eval-humming-b200-default` | one job | 3 |\n| `lm-eval-humming-h100-default` | 3 config-list shards | 5 |\n| `lm-eval-spec-decode-4xb200` | one job | 3 |\n| `amd-entrypoints-integration-api-server-generate` | one job | 2 |\n| `amd-entrypoints-integration-api-server-openai-chat_completion` | one job | 2 |\n\n- Every test and every config runs exactly once across the shards, with the same environment as before.\n- A config added later to `humming/config-h100.txt` is placed by the planner rather than by editing a shard list.\n- The two AMD mirrors' base steps are already enrolled; a native mirror shards only when its own `mirror.amd` sets the flag too.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Planned each step with ci-infra main's `runtime_shard.plan_step` against this branch and main's timings.\n- Rendered each step and mirror with the pipeline generator and ran every shard's commands against a mock test tree, as a PR build and with main's `pytest -p ci_otel` wrapper. Every test of every command ran in exactly one shard, with the same env (6/6 NVIDIA cases, 4/4 AMD mirror cases).\n- For the three gsm8k steps, also ran the generated shard jobs against vLLM's real `tests/evals/gsm8k/conftest.py` and config lists, with the test body stubbed. All 14, 15 and 3 configs ran once each, and the plan matched every config ID main has timings for.\n- `/ci run` covers the two AMD mirrors. The three LM eval steps are optional, so a build scoped to their keys follows the `/ci run` build.\n\n## Details\n\n- **`lm-eval-spec-decode-4xb200`** has three configs, one per shard. The longest is about the 20-minute target on its own, so the planner flags the step over budget. The wait still roughly halves.\n- **Planner estimates for eval steps.** The planner counts test time, not the model load and server start that each shard repeats. #62120's H100 MoE Refactor step came in longer than planned for this reason, so `lm-eval-humming-h100-default` (5 shards of about 3 configs) is the one to watch in the scoped build.\n- **`caplog`.** Two files in the Generate mirror assert on log text; the same files already run sharded in the enrolled NVIDIA step on main.\n- **Timeouts unchanged (human).** As in #62077 and #62120, `timeout_in_minutes` stays per job. A single-job fallback (no plan, or the `VLLM_CI_RUNTIME_SHARD` rollback switch) would outrun it; fallback-aware timeouts belong in a central timeout setting.\n- **Left out.** The spec decode AMD mirror runs under 20 minutes as one job. #61473 also touches two of these steps; it continues as #61831, which doesn't include them.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
